      "type": "text",
      "role": "hook",
      "headline": "Rome moved rivers’ worth of water for miles with no pumps at all."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.072.c2",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Downhill all the way",
      "body": "Aqueducts carried water by gravity, along a gentle, steady downhill slope from springs in the hills. Most of an aqueduct’s length ran underground or at ground level; the famous arches carried it across valleys."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.072.c3",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Water for a city",
      "body": "Rome’s first aqueduct, the Aqua Appia, was built in 312 BCE by Appius Claudius. By the 3rd century CE, 11 aqueducts supplied the city, feeding fountains and the great public baths. Frontinus, Rome’s water commissioner around 97 CE, wrote a book about them."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.072.c4",
      "type": "fact",
      "fact": "The Pont du Gard still stands in southern France.",
      "context": "This three-tier aqueduct bridge rises about 49 m above the river it crosses."
    },
